EOL proof for this commit - run 2026-11-18, after adding tools/live-probe/.gitattributes

Repo state that made this necessary: core.autocrlf=true, no root .gitattributes, every file
in this folder previously untracked. Under that config a fresh checkout smudges LF blobs
to CRLF, which changes byte length and therefore breaks the sha256 pin that the harness
enforces on every run and that this folder's prose publishes.

git ls-files --eol (i/ = index blob, w/ = worktree):

Decisive test - git checkout-index of the staged blobs into an empty folder, then hash what
a clone would actually put on disk:
  AutoStemProbe/__init__.py   26112 B  f34292cf12df8d27a0bc235da5870837abe143509a9a267f2b03950542299c29  CR=0  == published
  probe-harness.py            49264 B  a5e991884f508209801581928e033a27184cf9886bcc6ae2c7951ff0514d3d7c  CR=0  == published

Without .gitattributes the same extraction yields 26662 B / 49264+1020 CRs and the harness
exits 2 PIN_MISMATCH on a clean clone.

Five files carry CR bytes in the working tree (mixed endings from bash+python capture):
  acceptance-2p-1656/seam-read.out(5) acceptance-repin-f34292cf/12-g0-snapshot-verify.log(7)
  instantiation-gate-green.log(21) instantiation-gate-red-final.log(15) instantiation-gate-red.log(15)
Their staged blobs are LF-normalised by text=auto, so the committed record is deterministic
even though this desk's copies stay mixed until a re-checkout. None of the five publishes
its own hash, so normalising them invalidates nothing.

Staging excluded AutoStemProbe/__pycache__/__init__.cpython-312.pyc: a compiled artifact
written 2026-11-15 20:25 by a Python 3.12 interpreter, over bytes two generations older than
the current pin. Not evidence, and not the pinned artifact either.
